  "conclusions": [
    "Reviewer is below both fair and local ceilings; fair allocation is not its blocker.",
    "Managed sweep tasks bypass the fair ceiling; there is no FIFO, aging or reservation for the waiting reviewer.",
    "Six other sweep starts occurred while the reviewer waited; this is not a period with no released openings.",
    "Several openings were refilled within roughly 8, 76 and 50 milliseconds of another worker logging release, much shorter than the reviewer two-second retry interval.",
    "Other openings coincided with reviewer memory-pressure or cooldown holds; one documented remaining opening reached shared staggered startup then was claimed by a sweep worker.",
    "Fresh identity and heartbeat observations show a functioning queue. Repeated capacity losses create starvation risk; these observations do not prove indefinite starvation."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Resource status writes are throttled to roughly five seconds; they do not record every two-second attempt.",
    "Release status is written immediately after shared release, so measured intervals are approximate, not exact lock acquisition durations.",
    "Admission observer samples roughly once per second and can miss sub-second vacancies.",
    "Current participant demand is inferred only for the single reviewer; the scheduler fair formula counts registered participants and does not store demand."
  ]
}
